<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuoba Shiyijian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">320–376</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> Shiyijian was prince of Dai, the Tuoba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> state in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, from 338 to 376. Having spent years as a hostage at the Later Zhao court, he introduced officials, written laws and a more settled capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengle">Shengle</a>. When Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> under Fu Jian invaded in 376, he died amid violence within his family and Dai was divided. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Tuoba Gui</a> later founded the Northern Wei.</p></div>
